Date: Fri, 24 Sep 1993 19:49:08 -0400
From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: Meaninglessness

My original question was about MCP's use of the word 'meaningless.' He
seemed to think that because a certain Randian statement ('existence
exists') is wooly and non-operational, that it was also therefore
'meaningless.' I responded because I thought this was a bit too much of a
jump. I think Randians understand each other, and that if we put sufficient
effort into it we may understand them, even if we continue to think that
the statements they make are wooly and non-operational. I would give the
chirping of birds as an example of true meaninglessness (though even these
may have some rudimentary meaning, where mating sounds are distinct from
territory challenges, etc.).

I don't think MCP is dumb, so I must not be expressing myself clearly. I
have no idea whether he understands my objection, or whether he thinks that
I think operationalism is useless or I'm trying to defend Rand. He
apparently thinks that in some kinds of talk, even when both listener and
(sometimes) audience think they understand each other, there is really an
absense of meaning; but in other cases, even though the talk is rather
ordinary and not self-consciously operationalized, that there is meaning;
and that operationalism defines the line between these two kinds of talk.

I don't understand how this line is drawn. I'm perfectly comfortable
thinking that operationalism has some valuable uses, but I don't see how it
can be applied to every type of human discourse, nor how those types of
discourse that may not cut it can be arbitrarily silenced. Operationalism
can be used to improve understanding, as when an operational procedure or
definition replaces a non operational one; or it can be used to attempt to
stifle discussion when no alternative is offered. I oppose the latter.

MCP has not simply claimed that he thinks 'existence exists' is wooly and
not worth bothering with, but has claimed it is meaningless. I claim that
the only way to make that statement true is to finagle the definition of
'meaningless.'

Here is my test for meaning. Take two persons who think they understand
each other. Have them each separately talk to a third person until the test
subject is satisfied that the other understands the topic. Then ask the
third person whether the first two understand each other (not whether he
agrees with them). Perhaps this is too subjective to rate the title
operational. Also, it's rather trivial, since only lunatics would ever fail
it. It is obviously distinct from MCP's test, although I don't know what
that is.

At  9:00 AM 9/24/93 +0000, Michael Clive Price wrote:
>I regard [mental states, opinions etc] as operational, since, in principle,
>they correspond to brain states - 

So how do you rule out any statement?

>I'm perfectly happy to talk to
>people using the common pool of shared concepts and words.  It's where
>disagreements about what concepts mean that I fall back on
>operationalism to clarify meaning.

You did not clarify 'existence exists,' you dismissed it.

>>> The test is for you to define a concept _without_ defining it
>>> operationally.  Have a go.  I don't believe it can be done.
>> 
>> What is the operational definition of "novel" [book or new?] or
>> "Picasso's Blue period?"
>
>Can't see the problem, but the test was for you to [define a concept _without_
>>defining it operationally], and then we'll see if they're operational.

I could pursue this, because I think there are plenty of ordinary words
that are not defined operationally. "Novel" (book) and "Picasso's Blue
period" were two examples. But that is peripheral to my point. Even if I
fail to come up with a word that I can define non-operationally that you
can't define better operationally, that says nothing about the
meaninglessness of 'existence exists.' 

I'm claiming that by your criteria, "novel" and "Picasso's Blue period" are
meaningless. If you think they are defined operationally, I don't
understand at all. Should we feel unsatisfied if you can't come up with an
operational definition of either? Should we stop using those words?

>Newton was quite clear that we all we know of
>objects is through their interactions.  I quote the Great Man:
>     "... the qualities of bodies are only known to us by
>     experiments ... we are certainly not to relinquish the
>     evidence of experiments for the sake of dreams and vain
>     fictions of our own devising..."

So his position on the qualities of bodies is clear. How about philosophy
and theology?

>I've just been reading that Newton's
>language, even in his private notes, was almost entirely devoid of
>adjectives and metaphors....

Did he anywhere claim that using adjectives or metaphors was meaningless?
Newton's formulation of physics certainly was better than his
predecessors', but that doesn't mean theirs was meaningless.

>Great scientists often show an impatience with wooly questions, even (or
>especially) from laymen.
>
Impatience is one thing. Did they claim that such questions were
incomprehensible, or merely that the time and effort required to
reformulate the quesstions and educate the questioners was not worth
expending?

>> I would be gratified to find out about some operationally defined
>> metaphysics.
>
>That's exactly what's happening now.

I'd appreciate a more specific answer, since it's not obvious to me how to
apply operationalism to the question of identity through change, etc.

