Subject: Crypto-Anarchy & Libertarian RealPolitik

At  2:38 AM 9/23/93 -0700, Nick Szabo wrote:
>* There are many subtle problems with the "web of trust": the
>need to trust central banks not to track digicash transactions

I don't understand that particular point, but I generally agree. 

Another problem with crypto-anarchy lies in the difference between black
markets and white markets. The state never can eliminate markets entirely,
but they can effectively eliminate some important parts of markets that
depend upon openness. Guarantees, advertizing, and return policies, for
example, suffer in black markets. A black market price is still a market
price, but it may lack some of the information transmission properties we
associate with ordinary markets, due to the fact that market participants
need to keep their operations secret.

Such secret-keeping works better on networks, so CA may improve on physical
black markets by allowing anonymous pseudonyms with reputations etc. But I
doubt that even information markets will be what we would like them to be
in an environment of state hostility. My best hope is that crypto-anarchy
will make states come to some reasonable compromise with the inevitable.
There may be some advantage to being the nightwatchman-state (mall manager)
of a thriving information economy rather than the tyrant of an outdated
industrial society sliding down the tubes.

