Date: Sat, 25 Sep 1993 1:18:04 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: it's not the net we were promised, but ...

By way of reinserting myself into the list (after nine months in the
other world), I've got a question to throw open.

If you're on this list, by definition you're familiar with the idea of
the internet (even if only at its most basic level -- store and forward
file transfer and email). The net's obviously growing very fast right
now. What are the likely near-term limits to growth, and what new 
services do you expect to see emerging over the next, say, five years?

I'm particularly interested in this time span, because if the current
exponential curve continues a simple extrapolation suggests that the internet
will become the dominant communications medium in the United States
shortly after this period, and in Europe and the rest of the developed
world maybe three to five years after _that_. This impact will be shaped
by the then-prevalent uses to which the net is put. For example, the old
ARPAnet served mostly for file transfer and mail. More recently, the Internet
and NSFnet (and everything else with a TCP/IP link) began shunting batched
news around, then providing remote login services and more outre applications
like gopher, WAIS, and World Wide Web.
 
Where do _you_ think we're going? What is the net going to look like
when it finally erupts into public view, and how will it compare with its
present form?

(Obligatory comment: I'm trying to provoke a discussion here. I have some
ideas of my own, but I'm keeping them to myself until I see whether anyone
wants to discuss this ...)


