Date: Thu, 23 Sep 1993 17:54:53 -0400
From: Harry Shapiro <habs@panix.com>
Subject: SPACE: limits on launch costs

I would like to respond to several points raised by Mr Freeman,
that apparently were discussed at an Extropian luncheon:
 
" [about big towers] I can see great scientific
 use for a tower with a small payload, even if it were only 100 Km
tall.  "

One of the things that led me to think about such towers was the
NASA proposal for a successor to the Kuiper Airborne Observatory.
The next generation concept that NASA was thinking about is called
SOFIA, and consisted of a modified 747 carrying a large telescope,
flying at about 12-13 km.  A tower that high could carry a larger
telescope, have a higher duty cycle, and cost less to buy and
operate.

Technical issues:

    (1)  Maintainability.  How does one design the structure to
         allow easy repair at (say) the 50 Km level.

I can think of two ways.  The tower is made of many small truss
elements.  One could have a remote controlled robot which includes
a jack to take over the load of a truss element so you can remove
and replace it.  Alternatively, you could have a human in a can
supplying heat and breathing air working through a glove box
arrangement.  

    (2)  Sensitivity to low-frequency vibrations, as in an
         earthquake.

Some tall office buildings in earthquake regions have shock absorbers
to damp out earthquake vibrations at the resonant freqency of the
building.  In one example I saw, it took up most of a floor of a
building.  Also, a 10 meter movement of the ground is not much
flex in a 300 km tower.  I don't have any pat answer, but civil
engineers have design rules for earthquake regions, and I would expect
you would just follow them.

    (3)  Loads imposed or exacerbated by ice and snow.  If the
         combination of tower characteristics and climate results
         in ice accumulation on girders and guy wires in the
         troposphere, there might be real problems, not only with
         extra weight but also with aerodynamics.

I dont know what the best solution for ice buildup will be.  One approach
is to simply design for it.  Antenna towers in areas prone to ice
require allowing for the weight and area increase due to ice.  So you
may need to build up the structure in the lower 10 km or so.  Another
approach is to install heaters in the truss elements, and run them
sequentially to remove the ice and snow. 

    (4)  Severe-storm wind loads -- hurricane, tornado,
         thunderstorm -- particularly in the context of (3).

The design rule I used followed what is standard civil engineering
practice.  It results in assuming 150 miles per hour winds at the
ground, going up linearly to 750 miles per hour at 10 km (where
the jet stream winds are strongest).  Tornadoes may reult in 
localized winds stronger than 150 mph on the ground, but most structures
are not designed to withstand this - you buy insurance instead.

    Interesting social/legal problems would include:

    (1)  What sticks up might fall down.  If you don't end up
         putting it where fall-down would be harmless, how do you
         deal with the possibility of death and damage to others
         in the event of a failure?

You design it to the requirements to make it an insurable structure,
and then get insurance (probably through Lloyd's given the size and
novelty).  A 747 crash landing in a big city would hurt a lot of
people too, but they manage to get insurance.

    (2)  By the way, just where do you put it?

For space launch, the preferred location throws payloads due east
on the equator, so the space bridge in orbit passes over every
orbit.  Thus you offset the tower from the equator by the length
of the rotating cable you fling payloads with (600 km in the
example I gave.  I suggest some Pacific atoll, where the depth for
the foundation wouldn't be too much.  Another alternative would
be the Australian outback, although that would be too far off the
Equator.  In any case, a 300 km tall tower was an example to show
what is physically possible.  10-20km towers are much more likely
to be built as a first step.

    (3)  If it sticks up a long way it will tend to intercept
         low-flying satellites.  Again there are legal
         repercussions (as well as physical ones!).  What would
         such a tower do to current law and practice concerning
         the boundaries of space and rights\\\\\\ entitlements to
         passage there.

    Interesting aspects of things to do with such a tower, besides
    use as a possible spacecraft launch site -- already discussed
    here -- would include:

    (1)  Very clean environment.  Stuff that outgasses from a
         satellite drifts along with you; stuff that outgasses
         from a tower falls down.
    (2)  Few power limitations.  You certainly have the option of
         running electrical cables up the structure, perhaps even
         of making them part of the structure.
    (3)  Access to zero-gee for intervals of several minutes, by
         dropping things down a central guy wire (and slowing them
         up at the bottom before they splat).
    (4)  Continuous scientific study of parts of the atmosphere
         presently quite difficult to monitor.
    (5)  Good place to do many kinds of space astronomy.
    (6)  Great for bungee jumping.

(7) Market opportunities:

* A 10 km tower on the north side of a metropolitan area could
re-use all the satellite frequencies of GEO satellites (the
satellites appear in the south from the US), thus providing
additional cable channels, etc.  Since you would be tens of km,
rather than tens of thousands of km away, home receivers could use
much smaller antennas than home satellite dishes use.  Since satellite
transmitters are on the order of 20W-100W in GEO, trivial amounts
of power are needed for the tower-based transmitter.  You could
use a microwave power-beam from the base of the tower up to
a small rectenna on the top to send hundreds of watts up the tower
without running an aluminum wire up the side.

* A network of towers above the cloud tops might be competitive
with fiber optic cables for cross country data transfer using
lasers/optical relay.  





