Date: Fri, 24 Sep 93 23:16:00 PDT
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: it's not the net we were promised, but ...

Charlie Stros:
> What are the likely near-term limits to growth, and what new 
> services do you expect to see emerging over the next, say, five years?

This makes a good jumping-off point.  
First, the hype that will underwhelm:

* Use of increasing bandwidth for video.  This is being 
promoted not due to user demand, but because turning 
the NSFNet into an Information Highway pork barrel requires the need 
for very high bandwidth "backbones".  Centralization is not 
justfied by widespread decentralized use of text, graphics or voice 
mail.

* Growth rate.  Most of the "60% per year" Internet
growth rate is just people joining from Usenet and various BBS
nets.  "The Net" will have less than 50 million regular 
users in 2000, nowhere near projections of 100 million - 1 billion
I've seen.  Still, 50 million is the population of a medium sized
European country, and with 50 million elite crammed into
one truly interactive global city, intellectual sparks will fly.
After a lull in the late 90's, growth rate will pick up again
as multimedia tools turn into something more like "virtual
reality", and as Third World net connections become cheap and
the "Nintendo generation" and its successors find themselves 
increasingly comfortable in cyberspace.  

* Bringing Internet into the home for the masses.  This will
be good for some kinds of small business and Internet junkies
like me, but not for the masses.  In the long run I see more
demand as virtual ethnic communities spring up over multimedia
Internet connections.

Trends that will overwhelm:

* Spread of Internet to Third World countries.  Look for 100,000's
of sites and millions of users in India and China in 2000. 

* Many engineers and most programmers will be working for an overseas 
employer by 2010, albeit describing an employer as "overseas" will have
become less meaningful.  

* English literacy will spread rapidly.  By 2010 nearly 50% of the 
planet's teenagers will be able to read and write English with skill 
comparable to a U.S. teenager, and practically all the world's young 
doctors, lawyers, engineers, etc. will be English literate.
There will also be a worldwide revival in the study of archaic
Latin and Greek.   Most of what a few elite foreign students now pay U.S. 
universities $10K's per year for will be avaiable to Third World
masses via Internet and pirate CD-ROM.  Hey, Hey, Ho, Ho, Western Civ 
is Gonna Blow!

* "Talk" and "IRC" bloom into a mind-numbing variety of net-shared 
interactive apps with text, voice, still and animated graphics.  Virtual 
whiteboard plus voice and muti-user CAD software will spread.  The
simple net-sharable interfaces of relational databases, source-code 
control systems, etc. get extended to the graphical world of engineering 
and artistic design.  MUDs with spreadsheets, on-line markets, etc. etc. 
etc.

Trends that will continue apace:

* Telecommuting for engineers will move into higher gear, although
it will mostly be baby boomers with babies who can take advantage
of it.  Crypto-telecommuting has huge potential.

* Internet commercialization.  Again this will mostly be Mom & Pop
BBS's already out there popping up on the radar screen.  However
since much of this is porn, pirate ware, bookies, and other 
fringy operations, expect it to duck back down into "BlackNet" type 
operations as various jurisdictions crack down in their typical piecemeal,
ham-fisted way.  Internet commercialization will get a huge
second wind when cheap multimedia machines start finding their
way on to the Net: client/server sons & daughters of 3DO.

* With any luck, crypto-anarchy will play a big role in 
Internet commercialization, especially in the lives of those
millions of programmers working for offshore companies.
Alas, I suspect that insecure operations, like people sending their 
credit card numbers all over the Internet, will dominate.  This will 
provide much fun for crackers, hacker-crackdowners, etc.

